<p style="text-align: center;"><img src="/images/2015/Bainbridge.jpg" alt="A plein air painting of Bainbridge Island" width="450" height="341" /></p>
<p>Bainbridge Island (plein air). 9x12, oil on linen panel. 2015</p>
<p>Today I managed to get back to the beach for my last plein air effort of 2015. It was less windy than the last outing but still fairly cold.</p>
<p>After my experiences on the first outing I made a knotted bag that can be filled with stones to keep the tripod stable (it attaches to an extending hook under the main mast).</p>
<p style="text-align: center;">&nbsp;&nbsp;<img src="/images/2015/Bainbridge-Setup.jpg" alt="Plein air painting on the beach at Ballard" width="350" height="467" /></p>
<p style="text-align: left;">When painting at the beach everything has to be secured to guard against a sudden gust of wind taking it into Puget Sound. The tool bag that I use to carry tubes of paint is very heavy so it makes a good anchor on which to tie loose things like the tripod bag. I keep a few loose pieces of cord in my bag just for that purpose.</p>
